wayland-server: the screen's display modes on wl_output, and a way to set one

wl_output sent only the current mode, so a client could not know the screen's
other modes: Wine emulated a game's display mode by scaling its windows on the
CPU every frame (a 640x480 Direct3D game full screen: ~22 fps on a Raspberry Pi
4, against ~140 with the screen really at 640x480, whose display hardware
scales the mode to the monitor).

- wl_output sends the screen's sizes: each mode of its list once, at the
  refresh rate closest to 60 Hz, and the small sizes old games ask for
  (320x200 to 1024x768; the screen takes any size up to its list's largest),
  the current one flagged, the one at the first bind preferred.
- wl_ips_haiku_set_mode(width, height, refresh_mHz), exported for Wine's
  Wayland driver (an in-process client): sets that size (a mode of the list,
  else the current mode's timing at that size) as a temporary mode, which
  app_server undoes when the process quits; refits the fullscreen toplevels to
  the new screen and sends the new current mode to every bound wl_output. Runs
  on the server's thread (a message to the server handler from any other).

